        listening on.

        These will contain IP addresses, not hostnames, and will also indicate
        whether the given listener is http or https.

        Note that if you used Server.listen_all() the returned URIs will use
        the addresses `0.0.0.0` and `::`, rather than actually returning separate
        URIs for each interface on the system.

        Returns:
            a list of GLib.Uri, which you
                must free with each element with GLib.Uri.unref() when you are done with it.
        """
    def is_https(self) -> _bool:
        """Checks whether self is capable of https.

        In order for a server to run https, you must call
        Server.set_tls_certificate(), or set the
        Server.props.tls_certificate property, to provide it with a
        certificate to use.

        If you are using the deprecated single-listener APIs, then a return value of
        True indicates that the Server serves https exclusively. If you are
        using Server.listen(), etc, then a True return value merely indicates
        that the server is *able* to do https, regardless of whether it actually
        currently is or not. Use Server.get_uris() to see if it currently has
        any https listeners.

        Returns:
            True if self is configured to serve https.
        """
    def listen(self, address: Gio.SocketAddress, options: ServerListenOptions) -> _bool:
        """Attempts to set up self to listen for connections on address.

        If options includes Soup.ServerListenOptions.HTTPS, and self has
        been configured for TLS, then self will listen for https
        connections on this port. Otherwise it will listen for plain http.

        You may call this method (along with the other "listen" methods)
        any number of times on a server, if you want to listen on multiple
        ports, or set up both http and https service.

        After calling this method, self will begin accepting and processing
        connections as soon as the appropriate GLib.MainContext is run.

        Note that this API does not make use of dual IPv4/IPv6 sockets; if
        address is an IPv6 address, it will only accept IPv6 connections.
        You must configure IPv4 listening separately.

        Args:
            address: the address of the interface to listen on
            options: listening options for this server

        Returns:
            True on success, False if address could not be
                bound or any other error occurred (in which case error will be
                set).
        """
    def listen_all(self, port: _int, options: ServerListenOptions) -> _bool:
        """Attempts to set up self to listen for connections on all interfaces
        on the system.

        That is, it listens on the addresses `0.0.0.0` and/or `::`, depending on
        whether options includes Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV4_ONLY,
        Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV6_ONLY, or neither.) If port is specified, self
        will listen on that port. If it is 0, self will find an unused port to
        listen on. (In that case, you can use Server.get_uris() to find out
        what port it ended up choosing.

        See Server.listen() for more details.

        Args:
            port: the port to listen on, or 0
            options: listening options for this server

        Returns:
            True on success, False if port could not be bound
                or any other error occurred (in which case error will be set).
        """
    def listen_local(self, port: _int, options: ServerListenOptions) -> _bool:
        """Attempts to set up self to listen for connections on "localhost".

        That is, `127.0.0.1` and/or `::1`, depending on whether options includes
        Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV4_ONLY, Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV6_ONLY, or neither). If
        port is specified, self will listen on that port. If it is 0, self
        will find an unused port to listen on. (In that case, you can use
        Server.get_uris() to find out what port it ended up choosing.

        See Server.listen() for more details.

        Args:
            port: the port to listen on, or 0
            options: listening options for this server

        Returns:
            True on success, False if port could not be bound
                or any other error occurred (in which case error will be set).
        """
    def listen_socket(self, socket: Gio.Socket, options: ServerListenOptions) -> _bool:
        """Attempts to set up self to listen for connections on socket.

        See Server.listen() for more details.

        Args:
            socket: a listening Gio.Socket
            options: listening options for this server

        Returns:
            True on success, False if an error occurred (in
                which case error will be set).
        """
    def pause_message(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None:
        """Pauses I/O on msg.

        This can be used when you need to return from the server handler without
        having the full response ready yet. Use Server.unpause_message() to
        resume I/O.

        This must only be called on a ServerMessage which was created by the
        Server and are currently doing I/O, such as those passed into a
        ServerCallback or emitted in a Server.request_read
        signal.

        Deprecated since 3.2: Use Soup.ServerMessage.pause() instead.

        Args:
            msg: a Soup.ServerMessage associated with self.
        """
    def remove_auth_domain(self, auth_domain: AuthDomain) -> None:
        """Removes auth_domain from self.

        Args:
            auth_domain: a Soup.AuthDomain
        """
    def remove_handler(self, path: _str) -> None:
        """Removes all handlers (early and normal) registered at path.

        Args:
            path: the toplevel path for the handler
        """
    def remove_websocket_extension(self, extension_type: _GTypeLike) -> None:
        """Removes support for WebSocket extension of type extension_type (or any subclass of
        extension_type) from self.

        Args:
            extension_type: a GObject.Type
        """
    def set_tls_auth_mode(self, mode: Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode) -> None:
        """Sets self's Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode to use for SSL/TLS client authentication.

        Args:
            mode: a Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode
        """
    def set_tls_certificate(self, certificate: Gio.TlsCertificate) -> None:
        """Sets self up to do https, using the given SSL/TLS certificate.

        Args:
            certificate: a Gio.TlsCertificate
        """
    def set_tls_database(self, tls_database: Gio.TlsDatabase) -> None:
        """Sets self's Gio.TlsDatabase to use for validating SSL/TLS client certificates.

        Args:
            tls_database: a Gio.TlsDatabase
        """
    def unpause_message(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None:
        """Resumes I/O on msg.

        Use this to resume after calling Server.pause_message(), or after
        adding a new chunk to a chunked response.

        I/O won't actually resume until you return to the main loop.

        This must only be called on a ServerMessage which was created by the
        Server and are currently doing I/O, such as those passed into a
        ServerCallback or emitted in a Server.request_read
        signal.

        Deprecated since 3.2: Use Soup.ServerMessage.unpause() instead.

        Args:
            msg: a Soup.ServerMessage associated with self.
        """
    def do_request_aborted(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None: ...
    def do_request_finished(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None: ...
    def do_request_read(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None: ...
    def do_request_started(self, msg: ServerMessage) -> None: ...
    @property
    def raw_paths(self) -> _bool:
        """If True, percent-encoding in the Request-URI path will not be
        automatically decoded.
        """
    @property
    def server_header(self) -> _str:
        """Server header.

        If non-None, the value to use for the "Server" header on
        ServerMessages processed by this server.

        The Server header is the server equivalent of the
        User-Agent header, and provides information about the
        server and its components. It contains a list of one or
        more product tokens, separated by whitespace, with the most
        significant product token coming first. The tokens must be
        brief, ASCII, and mostly alphanumeric (although "-", "_",
        and "." are also allowed), and may optionally include a "/"
        followed by a version string. You may also put comments,
        enclosed in parentheses, between or after the tokens.

        Some HTTP server implementations intentionally do not use
        version numbers in their Server header, so that
        installations running older versions of the server don't
        end up advertising their vulnerability to specific security
        holes.

        As with Session.props.user_agent, if you set a
        Server.props.server_header property that has trailing
        whitespace, Server will append its own product token (eg,
        `libsoup/2.3.2`) to the end of the header for you.
        """
    @server_header.setter
    def server_header(self, value: _str) -> None: ...
    @property
    def tls_auth_mode(self) -> Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode:
        """A Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode for SSL/TLS client authentication."""
    @tls_auth_mode.setter
    def tls_auth_mode(self, value: Gio.TlsAuthenticationMode) -> None: ...
    @property
    def tls_certificate(self) -> Gio.TlsCertificate:
        """A [class@Gio.TlsCertificate[] that has a
        Gio.TlsCertificate.props.private_key set.

        If this is set, then the server will be able to speak
        https in addition to (or instead of) plain http.
        """
    @tls_certificate.setter
    def tls_certificate(self, value: Gio.TlsCertificate) -> None: ...
    @property
    def tls_database(self) -> Gio.TlsDatabase:
        """A Gio.TlsDatabase to use for validating SSL/TLS client
        certificates.
        """
    @tls_database.setter
    def tls_database(self, value: Gio.TlsDatabase) -> None: ...

ServerCallback = _Callable[..., None]
"""A callback used to handle requests to a Server.

path and query contain the likewise-named components of the
Request-URI, subject to certain assumptions. By default,
Server decodes all percent-encoding in the URI path, such that
`"/foo%2Fbar"` is treated the same as `"/foo/bar"`. If your
server is serving resources in some non-POSIX-filesystem namespace,
you may want to distinguish those as two distinct paths. In that
case, you can set the Server.props.raw_paths property when creating
the Server, and it will leave those characters undecoded.

query contains the query component of the Request-URI parsed according to
the rules for HTML form handling. Although this is the only commonly-used
query string format in HTTP, there is nothing that actually requires that
HTTP URIs use that format; if your server needs to use some other format, you
can just ignore query, and call Message.get_uri() and parse the URI's
query field yourself.

See Server.add_handler() and Server.add_early_handler()
for details of what handlers can/should do.
"""

class ServerClass:
    padding: _list[_Any]
    parent_class: GObject.ObjectClass

class ServerListenOptions(GObject.GFlags):
    """Options to pass to Server.listen(), etc.

    Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV4_ONLY and Soup.ServerListenOptions.IPV6_ONLY
    only make sense with Server.listen_all() and
    Server.listen_local(), not plain Server.listen() (which
    simply listens on whatever kind of socket you give it). And you
    cannot specify both of them in a single call.
    """
    HTTPS: ServerListenOptions
    """Listen for https connections rather
      than plain http.
    """
    IPV4_ONLY: ServerListenOptions
    """Only listen on IPv4 interfaces."""
    IPV6_ONLY: ServerListenOptions
    """Only listen on IPv6 interfaces."""

class _ServerMessageProps(GObject._ObjectProps):
    tls_peer_certificate: Gio.TlsCertificate
    tls_peer_certificate_errors: Gio.TlsCertificateFlags

class ServerMessage(GObject.Object):
    """An HTTP server request and response pair.

    A ServerMessage represents an HTTP message that is being sent or
    received on a Server.

    Server will create ServerMessages automatically for
    incoming requests, which your application will receive via handlers.

    Note that libsoup's terminology here does not quite match the HTTP
    specification: in RFC 2616, an "HTTP-message" is *either* a Request, *or* a
    Response. In libsoup, a ServerMessage combines both the request and the
    response.
    """
    props: _ServerMessageProps  # type: ignore[assignment]
    @property
    def accept_certificate(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage, Gio.TlsCertificate, Gio.TlsCertificateFlags], _bool]]:
        """Emitted during the msg's connection TLS handshake
        after client TLS certificate has been received.
        You can return True to accept tls_certificate despite
        tls_errors.
        """
    @property
    def connected(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted when the msg's socket is connected and the TLS handshake completed."""
    @property
    def disconnected(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted when the msg's socket is disconnected."""
    @property
    def finished(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted when all HTTP processing is finished for a message.
        (After ServerMessage.wrote_body).
        """
    @property
    def got_body(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted after receiving the complete request body."""
    @property
    def got_chunk(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage, GLib.Bytes], None]]:
        """Emitted after receiving a chunk of a message body.

        Note that "chunk" in this context means any subpiece of the body, not
        necessarily the specific HTTP 1.1 chunks sent by the other side.
        """
    @property
    def got_headers(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted after receiving the Request-Line and request headers."""
    @property
    def wrote_body(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted immediately after writing the complete response body for a
        message.
        """
    @property
    def wrote_body_data(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage, _int], None]]:
        """Emitted immediately after writing a portion of the message
        body to the network.
        """
    @property
    def wrote_chunk(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted immediately after writing a body chunk for a message.

        Note that this signal is not parallel to
        ServerMessage.got_chunk; it is emitted only when a complete
        chunk (added with MessageBody.append() or
        MessageBody.append_bytes() has been written. To get
        more useful continuous progress information, use
        ServerMessage.wrote_body_data.
        """
    @property
    def wrote_headers(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted immediately after writing the response headers for a
        message.
        """
    @property
    def wrote_informational(self) -> _BoundSignal[_Callable[[ServerMessage], None]]:
        """Emitted immediately after writing a 1xx (Informational) response."""
    def get_http_version(self) -> HTTPVersion:
        """Get the HTTP version of self.

        Returns:
